{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 1497 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "2f253b0721a55a09ca35b28a3660891881226906209307872f790a627ff6693b" :edb/db-before-t 1496 :edb/db-after-t 1497 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534809 50 #inst "2026-10-12T15:49:57.143Z" 13194139534809 17592186045417 true] [17592186115075 1071 "Let ![](images/38/38c4cba166fdfcd0954a18156946e41834a626cccee4baab976861dd23574c85.png) Find the row operation that, when applied to a $2\\times 2$ matrix $A,$ gives the result of the product $EA.$" 13194139534809 17592186045417 false] [17592186115075 1071 "Let ![](images/a9/a9f425a48a3ed8fabfce0eb1785ebdddc62d7dd3208ac302fd66a89b03e58cdb.svg) Find the row operation that, when applied to a $2\\times 2$ matrix $A,$ gives the result of the product $EA.$" 13194139534809 17592186045417 true] [17592186115076 1074 "We need to find an elementary matrix $E$ such that $EA$ is equivalent to applying the elementary row operation $R_1:=R_1 - R_2$ to $A.$\n\nTo find $E,$ we apply the given row operation to the identity matrix as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}-{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =E\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equivalent notation in terms of matrix multiplication is $EA\\mathbin{:}$\n![](images/11/11c345fde8fecb2e2a5459a0868ad8892e9067820de72f91601c1033858ca019.svg)" 13194139534809 17592186045417 true] [17592186115076 1074 "We need to find an elementary matrix $E$ such that $EA$ is equivalent to applying the elementary row operation $R_1:=R_1 - R_2$ to $A.$\n\nTo find $E,$ we apply the given row operation to the identity matrix as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}-{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =E\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equivalent notation in terms of matrix multiplication is $EA\\mathbin{:}$\n![](images/a4/a4a2b1035b3b5699ff3558ffa839fbd667ad6defaf30a158d628c7011ab97aa8.png)" 13194139534809 17592186045417 false] [17592186115077 1074 "First, let's find the elementary matrices corresponding to the two given elementary row operations.\n\n- The first elementary row operation is $R_1 \\leftrightarrow R_2.$ The corresponding elementary matrix can be found by applying this same elementary row operation to the identity matrix as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & ↔{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={E}_{1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The second elementary row operation is $R_2:=R_2+3R_1.$ The corresponding elementary matrix can be found by applying this same elementary row operation to the identity matrix as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={E}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo apply the first elementary row operation to the matrix $A,$ we multiply by $E_1$ on the left and get $E_1 A\\mathbin{:}$\n\n![](images/d3/d35cb368b7380f08e486757813138fa3f3332ed40a9b0e5faeab7a20c3119bef.png)\n\nThen, to apply the second elementary row operation to $E_1 A,$ we multiply by $E_2$ on the left and get $E_2 E_1 A\\mathbin{:}$\n\n![](images/c8/c87dbe6c81a670dd62d8a130d3085b2e9b36d5d3ad27d53bda571ec69b639689.png)" 13194139534809 17592186045417 false] [17592186115077 1074 "First, let's find the elementary matrices corresponding to the two given elementary row operations.\n\n- The first elementary row operation is $R_1 \\leftrightarrow R_2.$ The corresponding elementary matrix can be found by applying this same elementary row operation to the identity matrix as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & ↔{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={E}_{1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The second elementary row operation is $R_2:=R_2+3R_1.$ The corresponding elementary matrix can be found by applying this same elementary row operation to the identity matrix as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={E}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo apply the first elementary row operation to the matrix $A,$ we multiply by $E_1$ on the left and get $E_1 A\\mathbin{:}$\n\n![](images/ea/ead4157a19d725334c44a55903b52bd12d7627e73696c2100efee52340347468.svg)\n\nThen, to apply the second elementary row operation to $E_1 A,$ we multiply by $E_2$ on the left and get $E_2 E_1 A\\mathbin{:}$\n\n![](images/ba/ba70270430d6c5606e816c16c606c8f3940742a48c95b0ad8a33f6c935da8cf6.svg)" 13194139534809 17592186045417 true] [17592186115246 1038 #edb/ref 17592186436813 13194139534809 17592186045417 true] [17592186115246 1038 #edb/ref 17592186436815 13194139534809 17592186045417 true] [17592186115246 1038 #edb/ref 17592186436819 13194139534809 17592186045417 true] [17592186115246 1038 #edb/ref 17592186436820 13194139534809 17592186045417 true] [17592186115247 1038 #edb/ref 17592186436816 13194139534809 17592186045417 true] [17592186115247 1038 #edb/ref 17592186436818 13194139534809 17592186045417 true] [17592186115247 1038 #edb/ref 17592186436821 13194139534809 17592186045417 true] [17592186115247 1038 #edb/ref 17592186436822 13194139534809 17592186045417 true] [17592186115248 1038 #edb/ref 17592186436814 13194139534809 17592186045417 true] [17592186115248 1038 #edb/ref 17592186436817 13194139534809 17592186045417 true] [17592186115473 1050 420.0 13194139534809 17592186045417 true] [17592186116497 1064 "To compute the product of an elementary matrix and another matrix, one option is to perform the matrix multiplication as usual. However, there's an easier, quicker way.\n\nRemember that every elementary matrix is the result of applying an elementary row operation to the identity matrix. If we multiply another matrix by an elementary matrix, we're effectively applying the elementary row operation to the other matrix.\n\nFor example, suppose we are asked to find the product $EA$ where\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe elementary matrix $E$ comes from swapping the two rows of the identity matrix ($R_1 \\leftrightarrow R_2$). So, to compute the product $EA,$ all we have to do is swap the two rows of $A\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}8 & 6 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThis may seem almost too easy. But it's true! And we can verify it by computing the product the long way, using matrix multiplication. Indeed, we get the same result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}EA & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 & 4 \\\\ 8 & 6\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}8 & 6 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we say that $E \\cdot A$ is equivalent to applying to $A$ the elementary row operation $R_1 \\leftrightarrow R_2.$\n\n**Watch out!** It's important to multiply $A$ from the *left*. If we multiply $A$ by an elementary matrix from the *right*, we will perform a *column* operation on $A$ (not a row operation). In this case, multiplying $A$ by $E$ from the right will have the effect of switching the *columns* of $A,$ not the rows.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AE & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 4 \\\\ 8 & 6\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & 3 \\\\ 6 & 8\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534809 17592186045417 false] [17592186116497 1064 "To compute the product of an elementary matrix and another matrix, one option is to perform the matrix multiplication as usual. However, there's an easier, quicker way.\n\nRemember that every elementary matrix is the result of applying an elementary row operation to the identity matrix. If we multiply another matrix by an elementary matrix, we're effectively applying the elementary row operation to the other matrix.\n\nFor example, suppose we are asked to find the product $EA$ where\n![](images/25/25d4c31649834fe3ae42a701d49f6e4a31fa8626de1d9aeed0f98ccbbf4a57fb.svg)\n\nThe elementary matrix $E$ comes from swapping the two rows of the identity matrix ($R_1 \\leftrightarrow R_2$). So, to compute the product $EA,$ all we have to do is swap the two rows of $A\\mathbin{:}$\n![](images/49/497b76accb211bbe4253ee2094206f70e8856f9510db71f6367f255fcc565665.svg)\n\nThis may seem almost too easy. But it's true! And we can verify it by computing the product the long way, using matrix multiplication. Indeed, we get the same result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}EA & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 & 4 \\\\ 8 & 6\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}8 & 6 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we say that $E \\cdot A$ is equivalent to applying to $A$ the elementary row operation $R_1 \\leftrightarrow R_2.$\n\n**Watch out!** It's important to multiply $A$ from the *left*. If we multiply $A$ by an elementary matrix from the *right*, we will perform a *column* operation on $A$ (not a row operation). In this case, multiplying $A$ by $E$ from the right will have the effect of switching the *columns* of $A,$ not the rows.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AE & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 4 \\\\ 8 & 6\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & 3 \\\\ 6 & 8\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534809 17592186045417 true] [17592186436753 1080 "{R}_{1}:={R}_{1}-15{R}_{2}" 13194139534809 17592186045417 true] [17592186436753 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139534809 17592186045417 true] [17592186436753 1083 #uuid "041b2509-a556-5b7c-ab45-9ca5fd9c3cc1" 13194139534809 17592186045417 true] [17592186436754 1080 "[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot A" 13194139534809 17592186045417 true] [17592186436754 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139534809 175